        {
          "id": "nist_good_password::chunk-15",
          "content": "MFA comes in many forms, such as USB dongles, authenticator apps, push notifications or text message codes. When MFA is turned on, a hacker would need to not only compromise your password but also gain control of the second factor. This second factor is much harder to compromise since it is typically something physically in your possession, like your phone",
